# Cloudflare Pages reads this file. Netlify uses the same format. GitHub Pages
# ignores it, which is one more reason to prefer Pages -- see README.md.
#
# The policy is tight because it can be: this build has no backend, loads
# nothing from a CDN, and makes no network requests of its own once the page is
# loaded. Anything that tries to is a bug or an attack, and connect-src 'self'
# turns either into a visible console error rather than a silent exfiltration
# of scan data.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' blob: data:; connect-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-ancestors 'self'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), interest-cohort=()

# style-src needs 'unsafe-inline': the page carries its stylesheet inline and
# uses style attributes throughout. script-src does NOT, and must not -- there
# is no inline script anywhere, and keeping it that way is what makes the
# script-src rule worth having.
#
# THE THREE ADDITIONS FOR RUNNING THE MODEL
#
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval' lets the page compile WebAssembly. The name is alarming and
# the permission is not: it allows WASM compilation only, and specifically does
# NOT re-enable eval() or inline script. Without it the inference runtime cannot
# start at all. It is the narrow keyword that exists precisely so pages like
# this do not have to ask for 'unsafe-eval'.
#
# worker-src blob: lets the runtime start its worker threads, which it creates
# from a generated blob rather than a file on disk.
#
# COEP: require-corp, together with the COOP line above, makes the page
# cross-origin isolated, which is the precondition for SharedArrayBuffer and so
# for multi-threaded inference. It costs nothing here because the page already
# loads every byte from its own origin -- there is no third-party resource for
# it to break. If inference silently drops to a single thread after a hosting
# change, check that both of these still arrive.
